        <body>
            <div xml:id="d369">

                <pb facs="0667" n="597" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg597"/>

                <head>No. 368.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Astor to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1883-03-06"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 6, 1883</date>. (Received March
                        26.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 8.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that the
                    unveiling of a tablet commemorative of the sojourn in Rome of Samuel F. B.
                    Morse, the inventor of electric telegraphy, took place yesterday. The ceremony
                    was interesting as being the first time the honor has been accorded to the
                    memory of an American. A considerable assemblage was present, and addresses were
                    made by Mr. Placidi, representing the municipality, and Mr. D’Amico, director
                    general of telegraphs, representing the minister of public works. In reply I
                    tendered the thanks of my countrymen to the <pb facs="0668" n="598"
                        xml:id="pg598"/> Italian Government and the municipality of Rome for their
                    graceful recognition of American genius.</p>
                <p>The tablet is affixed to the house No. 17 Via dei Prefetti, and bears an
                    inscription of which the following is a translation: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>S. P. Q. R. In this house from the 20th of February, 1825, to January 5,
                            1826, resided Samuel Finley Breese Morse, the inventor of the electric
                            telegraph. He was born in Charlestown, April 27, 1791, and died in New
                            York April 2, 1872.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WILLIAM WALDORF ASTOR.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
